WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.435
-My next guest is an activist

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:04.937
who's featured in the new
documentary "Us Kids,"

00:00:04.937 --> 00:00:07.473
which is in theaters
and on demand this Friday.

00:00:07.473 --> 00:00:10.076
Please welcome X Gonzalez!

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:11.377
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:11.377 --> 00:00:13.779
Ha ha! Yeah!

00:00:13.779 --> 00:00:16.182
Oh, it's so good to see you.
You --

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:18.418
We've had Zoom guests before,
but I don't think anyone

00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:20.119
has ever Zoomed
from their dorm room.

00:00:20.119 --> 00:00:21.321
This is the first --

00:00:21.321 --> 00:00:22.989
-I know.
it's pretty nice, isn't it?

00:00:22.989 --> 00:00:24.590
Gives everybody
some nice memories.

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:25.958
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:25.958 --> 00:00:27.727
I have my artwork.
I've got my tie dye.

00:00:27.727 --> 00:00:31.197
-I want to ask you about "X."

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:33.733
-Yeah, so, I used to be --

00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:39.639
My name is Emma Gonzalez,
but I've decided to go by X now

00:00:39.639 --> 00:00:42.275
because I really don't want
people who don't know me

00:00:42.275 --> 00:00:43.710
assuming that they do know me

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.212
because of
the national narrative

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:48.948
or international narrative
that exists about me.

00:00:48.948 --> 00:00:50.716
Because I was
and still am a child,

00:00:50.716 --> 00:00:53.319
and I don't want people thinking
that they're my friends

00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:54.821
just 'cause they know my name,

00:00:54.821 --> 00:00:57.690
and it also got kind of
tied up into my gender.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.626
I realized that I'm not a girl
and that I don't like

00:01:00.626 --> 00:01:03.228
when people refer to me
with she/her pronouns

00:01:03.228 --> 00:01:05.531
or when they think of me
as a girl.

00:01:05.531 --> 00:01:08.868
And Emma as a name
just became such an identity

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:11.970
that I became
really disassociated with.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:16.342
And so I kind of took towards X
as a sort of, like,

00:01:16.342 --> 00:01:18.478
I'm re-claiming
my own identity,

00:01:18.478 --> 00:01:21.047
and if you see me,
don't assume that you know me.

00:01:21.047 --> 00:01:23.049
Just assume that you're
seeing, like, a person.

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:25.584
-Yeah, very good.
Good for you.

00:01:25.584 --> 00:01:28.087
Well, again, X, welcome.

00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:30.089
You're actually majoring
in activism.

00:01:30.089 --> 00:01:31.724
-Yes, yes.

00:01:31.724 --> 00:01:33.993
-Which I can't think of a more
perfect thing to major in.

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:37.897
Is that what you saw yourself
majoring in or no?

00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:39.231
-Yeah, when I was growing up,

00:01:39.231 --> 00:01:41.567
I really wanted to work at NASA,
you know,

00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:44.804
especially because I'm a Florida
native and NASA's here,

00:01:44.804 --> 00:01:46.739
and I really wanted to, like,
work at the desks

00:01:46.739 --> 00:01:48.174
and stuff
that you see in movies.

00:01:48.174 --> 00:01:50.743
But I think now that's just
got to be one of those, like,

00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:53.746
special-interest topics
that I'm just really into.

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:55.681
But, yeah,
I'm majoring in activism.

00:01:55.681 --> 00:01:58.885
I have been taking
some of the coolest classes

00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:00.853
with some of the coolest
professors at my college,

00:02:00.853 --> 00:02:02.421
New College of Florida.

00:02:02.421 --> 00:02:06.292
Come here if you're of college
age and want to go to college.

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:07.893
It's a really,
really cool school.

00:02:07.893 --> 00:02:10.797
I've been taking some radical
learning sort of classes,

00:02:10.797 --> 00:02:14.667
like manifestos, alternatives
to capitalism and socialism,

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:16.635
post-colonial literature
and theory,

00:02:16.635 --> 00:02:18.705
Black social
and political thought,

00:02:18.705 --> 00:02:20.874
global politics,
radical comics, like --

00:02:20.874 --> 00:02:22.175
I can't even name them all.

00:02:22.175 --> 00:02:25.311
They're so fascinating
and so, like --

00:02:25.311 --> 00:02:29.315
I feel like sometimes my skull
is being, like, cracked open,

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:32.685
and information is being
directly beamed into my brain.

00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:35.921
-I want to talk about, actually,
your documentary, "Us Kids."

00:02:35.921 --> 00:02:37.289
-Yes!
-This is --

00:02:37.289 --> 00:02:39.058
It follows you
and other activists

00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:41.427
organizing March For Our Lives

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:45.998
after surviving
a school shooting in 2018.

00:02:45.998 --> 00:02:48.767
The film premiered at Sundance,
and I think I read somewhere

00:02:48.767 --> 00:02:52.538
it got one of the longest
standing ovations in history.

00:02:52.538 --> 00:02:54.040
-Yeah,
in the history of Sundance.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:57.976
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:57.976 --> 00:03:00.379
-Can you talk a little bit
about some of the things

00:03:00.379 --> 00:03:03.516
March For Our Lives has done?

00:03:03.516 --> 00:03:05.385
-Yeah, I actually
kind of want to shout out

00:03:05.385 --> 00:03:07.219
some of the members
of March For Our Lives

00:03:07.219 --> 00:03:08.821
that are in the audience
right now.

00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:11.357
Maxwell Frost, Elena, Brianni,

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:14.493
Gaby Salazar, Mia, Crystal,
Matthew Hogenmiller,

00:03:14.493 --> 00:03:15.761
all my friends!

00:03:15.761 --> 00:03:17.964
-Welcome!
-I love you.

00:03:17.964 --> 00:03:20.332
-Love you back!
-Oh, they love you back!

00:03:20.332 --> 00:03:22.501
This is happening. I love this.

00:03:22.501 --> 00:03:24.570
-But, like, I was
one of the people who --

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:26.339
And you see it
in the documentary,

00:03:26.339 --> 00:03:28.708
and I highly recommend
you watch it.

00:03:28.708 --> 00:03:33.246
I was at, like, the merch table,
and we would be selling merch,

00:03:33.246 --> 00:03:35.113
like, March For Our Lives merch,
and all the money would go

00:03:35.113 --> 00:03:37.950
to the local areas that
we were in for those events.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.953
And, like,
so, people would come up to me,

00:03:40.953 --> 00:03:42.755
say hi to me, and would
take pictures and stuff,

00:03:42.755 --> 00:03:45.558
and then they'd move on
to the next person

00:03:45.558 --> 00:03:47.560
because it was such
a long line of people.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.695
And we did that
like three events a day,

00:03:49.695 --> 00:03:51.730
every day for 64 days,

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:54.200
and I only took
a half a day off.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.434
-Wow. That's what
I'm talking about.

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:56.969
-Which is fun to say.

00:03:56.969 --> 00:03:58.604
Cannot recommend
anybody else do that.

00:03:58.604 --> 00:04:00.105
Don't look at this movie
and think,

00:04:00.105 --> 00:04:01.374
"Hey, I should do that."

00:04:01.374 --> 00:04:05.311
-How old were you when
that shooting took place?

00:04:05.311 --> 00:04:07.713
-I was, like, 18?

00:04:07.713 --> 00:04:09.682
I had just turned 18.
I had just gotten my license.

00:04:09.682 --> 00:04:11.150
My driver's license.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:15.921
-I've seen some interviews
around you

00:04:15.921 --> 00:04:17.323
where people just go up to you

00:04:17.323 --> 00:04:20.293
because you became
the face at one point,

00:04:20.293 --> 00:04:23.496
the face of this whole
March For Our Lives.

00:04:23.496 --> 00:04:27.766
And people were just yelling
at a 17-year-old kid,

00:04:27.766 --> 00:04:30.503
and it just
must be tough for you.

00:04:30.503 --> 00:04:33.639
I just go like --
Good for you for just being --

00:04:33.639 --> 00:04:36.342
just logically going,
"Great. You can yell.

00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:39.044
But let's talk to each other
and let's have a conversation."

00:04:39.044 --> 00:04:40.879
-Yeah, thanks.

00:04:40.879 --> 00:04:44.216
I agree, though,
it was kind of unbelievable

00:04:44.216 --> 00:04:46.819
the amount of situations
that we were, like,

00:04:46.819 --> 00:04:48.954
"Okay, I'm going to come to this
with a rational head

00:04:48.954 --> 00:04:50.623
and be, like,
a violence interrupter.

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:52.491
I'm going to try.
I'm going to try and just

00:04:52.491 --> 00:04:54.427
have a real conversation
with a real person."

00:04:54.427 --> 00:04:56.028
And all these people
would come and protest

00:04:56.028 --> 00:04:57.663
our events and stuff,
and they would be like,

00:04:57.663 --> 00:04:59.264
"You don't know what
you're talking about!"

00:04:59.264 --> 00:05:01.801
And I'm like, "Do you know
what you're talking about?

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:03.703
What is the media
saying about us?

00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:06.038
What do you think
that we're trying to do?"

00:05:06.038 --> 00:05:08.073
And they would say,
"We think that you're trying

00:05:08.073 --> 00:05:10.209
to take our guns away,"
and I would be like,

00:05:10.209 --> 00:05:12.611
"Okay, well, first of all,
that's not true.

00:05:12.611 --> 00:05:14.413
We are not advocating
for taking guns away."

00:05:14.413 --> 00:05:15.915
March For Our Lives
is an organization

00:05:15.915 --> 00:05:18.484
that supports
the Second Amendment.

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:21.287
It's actually, in fact,
an organization

00:05:21.287 --> 00:05:25.892
that supports the regulation
of the militia that,

00:05:25.892 --> 00:05:27.827
you know, that is mentioned
in the Second Amendment.

00:05:27.827 --> 00:05:30.529
So, in actuality, we should
all be working together.

00:05:30.529 --> 00:05:32.231
We should not be fighting
with each other.

00:05:32.231 --> 00:05:33.933
And when we had these
conversations on the ground

00:05:33.933 --> 00:05:35.635
with these people,
everybody found out

00:05:35.635 --> 00:05:37.703
how much we all had in common
with each other,

00:05:37.703 --> 00:05:38.971
and, like, all the --

00:05:38.971 --> 00:05:40.973
It was basically,
like, a cease-fire.

00:05:40.973 --> 00:05:43.743
Nobody would argue anymore.
Nobody was screaming and stuff.

00:05:43.743 --> 00:05:46.279
We would just, like, vibe
and just leave.

00:05:46.279 --> 00:05:48.313
Everybody would, like --
-Yeah, such a proud moment,

00:05:48.313 --> 00:05:52.217
I think, for this country to
see you and everyone doing that.

00:05:52.217 --> 00:05:56.288
I want to show a clip.
Here is X Gonzalez in "Us Kids."

00:05:56.288 --> 00:05:58.323
Take a look at this.

00:05:58.323 --> 00:06:02.328
-You think you have enough in
the movement to keep it really?

00:06:02.328 --> 00:06:03.730
-Oh, yeah. Oh, yeah.
Absolutely.

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:05.264
I mean,
we have years and years more

00:06:05.264 --> 00:06:06.699
than these people
are gonna live,

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:09.401
so we have a lot of time
on our hands.

00:06:09.401 --> 00:06:12.538
♪♪

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:17.743
I'm in no way, like, hiding
under a rock or going dormant.

00:06:17.743 --> 00:06:19.512
I'm not going to be quiet.

00:06:19.512 --> 00:06:22.447
♪♪

00:06:22.447 --> 00:06:26.018
I'm majoring in activism.

00:06:26.018 --> 00:06:29.388
In school. That's my thing.

00:06:29.388 --> 00:06:33.459
I'm just doing things
on my own terms.

00:06:33.459 --> 00:06:35.594
-X Gonzalez.

00:06:35.594 --> 00:06:38.464
"Us Kids" is in theaters
and on demand this Friday.

00:06:38.464 --> 00:06:40.733
Thanks again for being here.
I appreciate it.

